{"version":"6.9","hitCount":21729,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQM5VJSg1Njc0MDU1NA==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%28protein+structure+prediction+alphafold%29&cursorMark=AoIIQM5VJSg1Njc0MDU1NA%3D%3D&resultType=core&pageSize=3&format=json","request":{"queryString":"(protein structure prediction alphafold)","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":3,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42862223","source":"MED","pmid":"42862223","doi":"10.1007/978-1-0716-5571-9_2","title":"Predicting RNA and Protein:RNA Complexes with AlphaFold: Possibilities, Limitations, and Practical Guidelines.","authorString":"Suskiewicz MJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Suskiewicz MJ","lastName":"Suskiewicz","firstName":"Marcin J","initials":"MJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3279-6571"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre de Biophysique Moléculaire, CNRS, Orléans, France. marcin.suskiewicz@cnrs-orleans.fr."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3279-6571"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"3081","journalIssueId":4291390,"dateOfPublication":"2027 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol","title":"Methods in molecular biology (Clifton, N.J.)","essn":"1940-6029","issn":"1064-3745","nlmid":"9214969","isoabbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-01-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"19-44","abstractText":"AlphaFold 3 (AF3) extends deep learning-based structural prediction beyond proteins to other biomolecules, but its reliability for RNA and protein:RNA complexes remains uncertain. This chapter examines AF3's applicability to these targets from both theoretical and practical perspectives. RNA differs fundamentally from proteins in ways that challenge AF3's framework: RNA structures are more conformationally heterogeneous and more condition-dependent. Furthermore, RNA-related training data in the Protein Data Bank are scarce and lack the diversity of protein data, limiting both structural prediction itself and the reliability of AF3's self-assessment confidence metrics. Benchmarking studies and individual case analyses suggest that, in the RNA realm, AF3 may function primarily through memorization of known structural motifs and homology-based modeling rather than genuine global de novo prediction; nevertheless, it performs competitively with established RNA prediction tools and can serve as an attractive hypothesis generator. Practical guidelines are offered for using AF3 with RNA-containing systems, emphasizing the importance of careful interpretation of confidence metrics, visual inspection, and experimental validation. Future improvements will likely require expanded RNA structural data, better exploitation of evolutionary information, and potentially alternative representations of RNA structure beyond rigid 3D models.","affiliation":"Centre de Biophysique Moléculaire, CNRS, Orléans, France. marcin.suskiewicz@cnrs-orleans.fr.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"RNA-Binding Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"RNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computational Biology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nucleic Acid Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Models, Molecular"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Software"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prediction Algorithms"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["RNA","RNA-binding proteins","Rna Structure Prediction","Protein–rna Interactions","Alphafold","Alphafold3","Protein–rna Structure Prediction"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA","registryNumber":"63231-63-0"},{"name":"Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"RNA-Binding Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-0716-5571-9_2"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-12","firstPublicationDate":"2027-01-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42972149","source":"MED","pmid":"42972149","pmcid":"PMC13728038","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13728038"]},"doi":"10.1007/s13659-026-00654-8","title":"From structure prediction to ligand-target recognition: expanding the reach of AlphaFold in natural product research.","authorString":"Xu G, Hu H, Zhao W, Liu Z, Wang T, Liu R, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xu G","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Guangrui","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu H","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Haifeng","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"China State Institute of Pharmaceutical Industry Co Ltd, Shanghai, 200232, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao W","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Weihui","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu Z","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Zhengxi","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang T","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Ting","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu R","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Rui","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Song","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China. liusong@wust.edu.cn."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4305050,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Prod Bioprospect","title":"Natural products and bioprospecting","essn":"2192-2209","issn":"2192-2195","nlmid":"101577734","isoabbreviation":"Nat Prod Bioprospect"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101","abstractText":"Natural products constitute a structurally diverse reservoir of bioactive molecules and remain an important source of lead compounds for drug discovery. However, the limited availability of structural information on pharmacological targets and biomolecular interactions continues to hinder mechanism elucidation and structure-guided optimization, particularly for complex natural products such as bioactive peptides, proteinaceous natural products, and natural molecular glues. Recent advances in AlphaFold have transformed structural biology by enabling high-confidence prediction of protein structures and biomolecular complexes, providing unprecedented opportunities for structure-guided natural product research. Beyond conventional structure-based virtual screening, emerging co-folding strategies have demonstrated considerable potential for improving early enrichment in natural product screening and for elucidating the binding mechanisms of complex natural products with their targets. This review summarizes recent advances in AlphaFold-assisted natural product research, including target structure prediction, hit and lead discovery, co-folding-enabled interaction analysis, mechanistic investigation of peptide- and protein-derived natural products, and the emerging potential of AlphaFold for natural molecular glue discovery. We further discuss current limitations and future directions in structure prediction and interaction modeling. Overall, AlphaFold is reshaping natural product research from lead discovery to mechanistic characterization, with particular promise for advancing macromolecular natural products and molecular glue-mediated therapeutics.","affiliation":"Institute of Pharmaceutical Process, Hubei Province Key Laboratory of Occupational Hazard Identification and Control, School of Medicine, Wuhan University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, 430065, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Provincial Innovation and Entrepreneurship Training Program for Undergraduate","grantId":"S202610488181","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Structure prediction","Peptides","Natural products","Protein–protein Interaction","Molecular Glues","Alphafold","Co-folding"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s13659-026-00654-8"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13728038"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13728038?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["uniprot","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42906387","source":"MED","pmid":"42906387","doi":"10.1016/j.ejmech.2026.119442","title":"AlphaFold-driven structure-based drug discovery: Capabilities, benchmarks and limitations for medicinal chemistry.","authorString":"Pathak M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pathak M","lastName":"Pathak","firstName":"Manish","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6272-9196"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, Faculty of Pharmacy, Swami Vivekanand Subharti University, Meerut, Uttar Pradesh, 250005, India. Electronic address: manishpharm01@gmail.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6272-9196"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"Pt 2","volume":"321","journalIssueId":4293107,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Eur J Med Chem","title":"European journal of medicinal chemistry","essn":"1768-3254","issn":"0223-5234","nlmid":"0420510","isoabbreviation":"Eur J Med Chem"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"119442","abstractText":"Structure-based drug discovery (SBDD) has become an indispensable tool of medicinal chemistry due to the possibility of rational drug design based on the three-dimensional structure of the biological target. Nevertheless, the use of SBDD technology has been restricted due to the scarcity of known experimentally solved structures of the proteins that are the targets of drug action, especially those which are difficult to characterize, poorly understood and associated with diseases. Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based protein structure prediction technologies, such as AlphaFold, have substantially expanded structural biology by generating high-confidence predicted structures for a large fraction of the known proteome, although prediction confidence and accuracy vary considerably by target and by region within a target. This review covers the aspects of SBDD approach, traditional methods of protein structure determination and limitations that have restricted the application of this methodology. Additionally, this review addresses the use of AlphaFold models in major drug discovery processes including target identification, binding site prediction, molecular docking, virtual screening, lead optimization and structure-activity relationships (SAR). The possible utilization of AlphaFold in different areas of medicine, including oncology, antimicrobial resistance, neurodegenerative diseases and targeted protein degradation, is mentioned. Moreover, the collaboration of AlphaFold with new computational technologies, such as machine learning, generative artificial intelligence, molecular dynamics and multi-omics analysis, is described. An evidence-graded decision framework specifies when predicted structures are likely to suffice for hit finding, SAR interpretation, selectivity optimization, fragment-based design and degrader design and where quantitative evidence is still lacking. Even though AlphaFold has substantially advanced the field for example, in a prospective screen against the G protein-coupled receptor (GPCR) TAAR1, docking to AlphaFold2 models gave a 60% hit rate versus 22% for a homology model and a workflow combining an AlphaFold model with generative chemistry against the kinase CDK20 yielded a compound with an IC50 of 33.4 nM and an approximately eightfold antiproliferative selectivity window between cell lines-problems related to protein flexibility, conformational changes of proteins due to ligands, binding affinity prediction and biological complexity cannot be neglected and predicted structures still require experimental validation before guiding synthesis.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, Faculty of Pharmacy, Swami Vivekanand Subharti University, Meerut, Uttar Pradesh, 250005, India. Electronic address: manishpharm01@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Molecular docking","Protein Structure Prediction","Virtual Screening","Structure-based Drug Discovery","Computational Drug Discovery","Alphafold","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejmech.2026.119442"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}